---
/**
 * Section 11 — operator-built.
 *
 * The brief is explicit that the founder is not to be framed as an AI expert
 * or a software developer. The credibility claim here is restaurant floor
 * time, and nothing else. No client names, no counts, no results — none have
 * been supplied.
 */
---

<section class="section section--ruled section--steel" id="operator-built">
  <div class="shell shell--wide">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="copy" data-reveal>
        <span class="tape">Operator-built</span>
        <h2>Built by someone who has worked the shift.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          MISE OPS comes out of running restaurant operations, not out of watching them from
          a laptop.
        </p>
        <div class="stack-m body">
          <p>
            Every procedure on this site is shaped by the same experience: opening
            locations, training crews, covering a station when somebody called off, and
            finding out the hard way which knowledge was never written down.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is why the method spends a whole stage on verification. Anyone can write a
            procedure. Getting a chef to agree which of three versions is the standard — and
            getting the crew to use it — is the part that actually changes how a restaurant
            runs.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also why nothing here asks your team to learn a new system. Cooks did not
            sign up for software. They will use something that gives them an answer faster
            than asking a manager, and they will ignore anything slower.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="principles" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="mono-caps principles__title">How we work</h3>
        <ul class="principles__list">
          <li>
            <strong>We come to you.</strong> The knowledge is on the floor, not in a meeting.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Your standard, not ours.</strong> We document how you want it done, then
            you approve it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Written for the person doing it.</strong> Plain language, in the language
            they work in.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Scoped before it starts.</strong> You know what is included and what it
            costs.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You own it.</strong> It keeps working whether or not you stay with us.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .split {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-s);
  }

  .body {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .principles {
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-l);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
    padding: var(--space-m);
  }

  .principles__title {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-s);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-xs);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .principles__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-s);
  }

  .principles__list li {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    line-height: 1.55;
    padding-left: var(--space-s);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-wash-2);
  }

  .principles__list strong {
    display: block;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  @media (min-width: 58rem) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: 1.3fr 0.7fr;
      gap: var(--space-2xl);
    }
  }
</style>
